.TH LATTICEWM 1 "2026-07-31" "LatticeWM 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
latticewm \- an extensible window manager for the river Wayland compositor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B river \-c
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B latticewm
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B LatticeWM
is a window manager for
.BR river (1),
written in Common Lisp.  It is started by river rather than run on its own:
river is the compositor, LatticeWM decides where the windows go.
.PP
Its layout model is a replaceable policy layer rather than the program itself.
The shipped layer is conventional \(em recursive splits, tabs, workspaces,
floating, minimise, multi-monitor.  Loading the
.B lattice
system on top turns each workspace into an infinite plane of cells addressed by
integer coordinate, with zoom, pan and a drawn map.
.PP
Every decision it makes is a generic function you can add a method to, and it
carries a live Lisp image, so a method added at a REPL takes effect on the
running desktop without a restart.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-config
Write a commented starter configuration to
.I ~/.config/latticewm/init.lisp
and exit.  Never overwrites an existing one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Start without reading the configuration file.  Use this when a change to it
has broken something.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-restore
Start with an empty layout instead of the one saved at the last exit.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Load the configuration file, report every problem in it, and exit non-zero if
there were any.  Run this
.I before
logging out: a configuration error otherwise manifests as a failed session,
which is the worst possible moment to find one.
.TP
.BI \-\-eval " FORM"
Evaluate
.I FORM
inside an already-running LatticeWM, over its control socket, and print the
result.  May be given more than once, in which case the forms run in order.
See
.BR latticewm\-config (5).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-ipc
Do not open the control socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-swank\-port " N"
Start a SLIME/SWANK server on port
.IR N .
.B Off by default.
SWANK offers arbitrary code execution as the logged\-in user with no
authentication step; it is a superb development tool and a poor default for a
session binary.  When it is on it binds to the loopback interface \(em see
.B *swank\-interface*
in
.BR latticewm\-config (5).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
One of debug, info, warn, error or none.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " PATH"
Write the log to
.IR PATH ,
or to stderr alone if
.I PATH
is \(oq\-\(cq.  The default is
.IR ~/.local/state/latticewm/latticewm.log ,
rotated at two megabytes, because a window manager started from a login screen
has a stderr nobody can find.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-options
Print every configuration value with its default and its documentation.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-commands
Print every command with its documentation.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-keys
Print every key binding.
.TP
.B \-\-extension\-surface
Print every policy generic that can be specialized, generated from the
running image.
.TP
.B \-\-container\-surface
Print every generic a new
.I container kind
must answer, generated from the running image.  This is the second of three
surfaces: the first changes what the window manager decides, this changes what
it can hold.
.TP
.B \-\-hooks
Print every hook, what its functions are called with, and when it fires,
generated from the running image.  The third surface: what the window manager
.IR notices ,
as against what it decides or what it can hold.
.SH KEYS
The complete keymap is drawn on screen by
.BR Super+/ ,
built from the live bindings, so it includes anything you added yourself.
A few worth knowing before that:
.TP
.B Super+Return
A terminal.
.TP
.B Super+x
Run a command by name.  Emacs's M\-x; Tab completes, and a command that takes
arguments asks for them.
.TP
.B Super+;
Evaluate a Lisp expression in the running window manager.
.TP
.B Super+?
Ask about something: b for every binding, k for a key, c for a command,
a to search, o for a configuration value, s to change one.
.TP
.B Super+h j k l
Move the focus left, down, up, right.  The arrow keys do the same.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/latticewm/init.lisp
The configuration file.  Plain Common Lisp, read in the LATTICEWM/USER package.
Errors in it are logged and do not stop startup.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/latticewm/state.lisp
The saved layout, written every few seconds while it is changing and restored
at startup.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/latticewm/latticewm.log
The log, rotated at two megabytes.  See
.BR \-\-log\-file .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/latticewm\-$WAYLAND_DISPLAY.sock
The control socket, mode 0600.  See
.BR \-\-eval .
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/latticewm/
Installed extensions, examples and documentation.  This directory is on ASDF's
registry at startup, so
.B (load\-extension "name")
in a configuration file finds anything installed here.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/latticewm/doc/
The generated references, the extension guide, and the licences \(em ours and
the SIL Open Font License the bundled Terminus font is under.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/latticewm/protocol/
The river protocol descriptions this binary was built against, and
.IR PINNED ,
which records the river release they came from.  LatticeWM refuses to start
against a river whose river_window_manager_v1 is a different version, and this
is how that refusal is diagnosed on a machine with no source tree.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration directory is looked for, if set.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the saved layout, the log and the first\-run marker go.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the control socket goes.
.TP
.B LATTICEWM_DATA
An extra directory to look for installed extensions in, ahead of every other.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run it nested inside an existing Wayland session, which is a real compositor
and a real protocol and needs no second machine:
.PP
.RS
.B river \-c latticewm
.RE
.PP
Change something on the running desktop, from a shell:
.PP
.RS
.B latticewm \-\-eval '(setf *gaps* 8)' \-\-eval '(relayout :force t)'
.RE
.PP
Check a configuration change without logging out to find out:
.PP
.RS
.B latticewm \-\-check\-config
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR latticewm\-config (5),
.BR river (1),
.BR sbcl (1)
.PP
.I doc/EXTENDING.org
in the source tree is the guide to changing it, with worked examples.
.SH AUTHOR
Shaul Khayyat.
